#!/bin/sh
# script/bench: the performance microbenchmarks (milestone 21). Two instruments:
#
#   script/bench             deterministic icount counts (TCG); the regression currency
#   script/bench --check     diff against bench/baseline-aarch64.txt; FAILS on >10% drift
#   script/bench --save --why "<reason>"
#                            rewrite the baseline (do it in the commit that moved the numbers).
#                            --why is REQUIRED and writes a `# why:` line into the file above the
#                            numbers; repeat it once per reason when one save covers several
#                            moves. Milestone 302 (a baseline records what it was saved
#                            against): a floor that records only a number cannot tell
#                            the next reader compiler drift from a regression, and twice in four
#                            days it did not.
#                            A save whose moves are accounted for also adds lines to
#                            bench/attributions.tsv, or the weekly chart reads its step as
#                            unexplained drift (calef's ruling on #2554, 2026-10-22 UTC).
#   script/bench --real      HVF: natively on the Apple Silicon core; per-core magnitudes, no gating
#   script/bench --real --smp  HVF on 4 harts; adds the multi-hart throughput bench (smp_throughput)
#                              and, with a RedoxFS disk attached, the filesystem ones: fs_read and
#                              milestone 38's four throughput phases (fs_seq_read and friends)
#   script/bench --keyboard  attach a virtio keyboard and press it over the QEMU monitor, for the
#                            keyboard_up and keyboard_event rows (milestone 947 (the virtio input
#                            device runs on `virtio-drivers`)); composes with --riscv and --x86,
#                            never gates. See notes/benchmarks/kernel-bench-harness.md, "The
#                            keyboard rows"
#
# **All three architectures, each against its own baseline.** With no architecture flag the
# instrument is aarch64 and the baseline is `bench/baseline-aarch64.txt`; `--riscv` and `--x86`
# select the other two and their own `bench/baseline-<arch>.txt`. The flags compose with
# everything above, so `--check` and `--save --why` mean the same thing on each:
#
#   script/bench --riscv --check    diff against bench/baseline-riscv64.txt. CI runs this on
#                                   every pull request, beside the aarch64 leg
#   script/bench --x86 --check      diff against bench/baseline-x86_64.txt
#   script/bench --x86 --save --why "<reason>"
#                                   rewrite that architecture's baseline, same rules
#
# `--real` is aarch64 only, by construction rather than by omission: it runs on
# Hypervisor.framework, which executes the host's own ISA.
#
# --real is single-hart by default so the primitive magnitudes are per-core (the cross-OS comparison
# reads them that way); --smp boots the whole machine for the aggregate-throughput bench.
# See design/roadmap/0021-benchmarks.md and notes/benchmarks.md.
#
# Name: recorded (DECISIONS §134 for the form, and milestone 63's name table for the word).
# Introduced 2026-07-23 with milestone 21. §134 lists this script by name in the verb half of the
# `script/` family, which is where a thing that runs the benchmarks belongs. Milestone 63 settled
# the word from the other side while naming `os_primitives_benchmarker`: "this tree already uses
# benchmark for the output", `bench/baseline.txt` holds the committed measurements, and the agent
# noun names the producer as distinct from the product. That is the recorded reason the directory
# and the script share a stem rather than an accident, and the two jobs the word does are the
# product and the act. It is not one of the Scripts to Rule Them All names. no architect has ratified
# it.
#
# BUGS
#
#   - **A recorded reason is per file, not per row.** `--why` writes `# why:` lines into the header,
#     so a save that moves twelve rows for one reason and one row for another records both at the
#     top and relies on prose to say which is which. Per-row notes do not survive: the save path
#     rebuilds the file from a fixed header plus one `name ticks iters` line per result, which is
#     how the hand-written correction above riscv64's `rfence_self` was destroyed on 2026-09-21.
#     Milestone 415 (sub-tripwire drift accumulates across
#     baseline saves) item 2 asks for the finer shape and keeps it. Name a counter in the reason's
#     own words meanwhile.
#   - **Nothing checks that a reason says anything.** `--save --why x` passes. The gate is that a
#     person had to type something and that the something is in front of the next reader.
#

set -e
# A heavy job: wait for one of the machine's heavy-job slots first (helpers/heavy_slot.py).
. "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh"
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

# This project's own QEMU, if `script/ci-qemu` has built one (helpers/qemu-path.sh says why
# PATH is the mechanism). A no-op on macOS and on any machine without that prefix.
. helpers/qemu-path.sh
exec cargo xtask bench "$@"
